# What must NEVER enter an image build context from this directory.
#
# THE REASON THIS FILE EXISTS is the same one the .gitignore beside it opens with: this directory is
# both the source tree AND, once copied to a server, a live stack directory. compose.yml builds every
# image with `context: .`, so on a server `docker compose --profile build build hub-image` tars up
# the whole stack dir and hands it to the docker daemon — goldens, the hub's git repo, every box's
# overlay, and data/claude, which holds the CLAUDE LOGIN. None of that is a build input.
#
# On a CI worker holding a fresh checkout this file changes nothing, which is exactly why the gap
# went unnoticed: the images that are built in CI never saw a populated data/. It bites the first
# time someone builds where the stack actually runs — which is the supported way to run a stack whose
# add-on nobody else builds for them.
#
# IT IS NOT A COPY OF .gitignore, and the difference is the one line that matters:
#
#     build-setup.sh is GITIGNORED (it is your file, made from build-setup.sh.example) but it MUST
#     be in the build context — Dockerfile.addon COPYs it, by the name SETUP_SCRIPT gives. Exclude
#     it here and every add-on build fails at the COPY.
#
# Same for anything else a Dockerfile reads: common-setup.sh, terminfo-ghostty, tmux.conf, hub/,
# box-bin/, box-broker/, skills/, muster-box.sh, entrypoint.sh, pinchtab-config.json.example. If you
# add an exclusion, check it against the COPY lines first.

# ---- live stack state (bind mounts). The big one is data/, and the dangerous one is data/claude. ----
data/
db-init/
boxes/

# ---- the stack's own credentials ----
git-identity/
.env
service-env
box-env

# ---- per-stack config, made from the *.example files or templated onto the server ----
mounts
port-forwards
hub-services/
compose.project.yml
compose.override.yml
claude-settings.json

# ---- repository furniture: read by developers and by CI, never by a Dockerfile ----
.git/
.github/
docs/
tools/
tests/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.swp
